# PXQU tier map 'pxqu-glm53flash-hq': glm5next routed experts, blocks 3..44 x 3 tensors.
# expert budget 85.00 GiB -> 84.98 GiB used, avg 2.398 bpw, hist {'pxq2': 108, 'pxq3': 18}
# The quality variant of pxqu-glm53flash-pxq2: same pxq2 floor, 5.06 GiB more budget spent
# on pxq3 for the 18 deepest ffn_down_exps. Every one of those bytes lands in HOST RAM on
# both of our card sets (the VRAM budget is already full at uniform pxq2), so this map is
# ~5.1 GiB more host traffic per token than the default -- about 2.7 extra block-equivalents
# on the CPU side. Take it only if a quality sweep says pxq2 on the deep down projections is
# the thing that hurts.
#
# blk.45 (NextN/MTP) is deliberately NOT in this map; the quantiser keep-list copies that
# whole block at source precision. See the note in pxqu-glm53flash-pxq2.tiers.
# Shapes are the real ones from the unsloth UD-Q2_K_XL GGUF header.
# Solved by a Lagrangian knapsack on a PROXY sensitivity (depth x kind), not a
# measured sens.json -- no sensitivity sweep exists for glm5next yet. Regenerate
# from a real imatrix before treating the assignment as final.
# The precision-sensitive tensors (indexer.*, indexer_compressor_*, hc_*, ssm_a,
# ssm_dt.bias, ssm_f_*, ssm_g_*, ssm_norm, attn_k_b, attn_v_b, *_norm) are NOT in
# this map and must be left at source precision by the quantiser's keep list.
# Consumed by llama-quantize --pxq-universal.
^blk\.3\.ffn_gate_exps\.weight$=pxq2
^blk\.3\.ffn_up_exps\.weight$=pxq2
^blk\.3\.ffn_down_exps\.weight$=pxq2
